      "filename": "12th century Airavatesvara Temple at Darasuram, dedicated to Shiva, built by the Chola king Rajaraja II Tamil Nadu India (4).jpg",
      "title": "File:12th century Airavatesvara Temple at Darasuram, dedicated to Shiva, built by the Chola king Rajaraja II Tamil Nadu India (4).jpg",
      "url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8a/12th_century_Airavatesvara_Temple_at_Darasuram%2C_dedicated_to_Shiva%2C_built_by_the_Chola_king_Rajaraja_II_Tamil_Nadu_India_%284%29.jpg",
      "descriptionUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:12th_century_Airavatesvara_Temple_at_Darasuram,_dedicated_to_Shiva,_built_by_the_Chola_king_Rajaraja_II_Tamil_Nadu_India_(4).jpg",
      "width": 6000,
      "height": 4000,
      "mime": "image/jpeg",
      "license": "CC BY 2.0",
      "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0",
      "artist": "Richard Mortel from Riyadh, Saudi Arabia",
      "credit": "Airavatesvara Temple at Darasuram, dedicated to Shiva, built by the Chola king Rajaraja II in the 12th century (118)",
      "source": "12th century Airavatesvara Temple at Darasuram, dedicated to Shiva, built by the Chola king Rajaraja II Tamil Nadu India (4)",
      "description": "One of the Hindu temples in Tamil Nadu which is a part of a UNESCO world heritage site.\nIt was built by the Chola king Rajaraja II (AD 1143-1173). It is smaller in size than the Brihadisvara temple at Tanjavur and Gangaikonda cholapuram temples.\nThe Airavatesvara Temple is highly ornate. It consists of a sanctum without a circumambulatory path and axial mandapas. The front mandapa is designed as a chariot with wheels. The pillars of the mandapa are intricately carved. \nIt is significant in Saiva Siddhanta tradition of Hinduism. The temple shows the Siva-related Bhakti movement, with friezes of the 63 Nayanars. There is a tradition of singing from Thevaram in this temple. \n\nA number of sculptures from this temple are now at Tanjavur Art Gallery. The temple is famed for its artwork related to Nagaraja, Agastya, dancing Nataraja in the form of Martanda Bhairava, Ganesha.",
      "dateCreated": "Taken on 31 August 2017, 16:23",
      "sourceType": "core",
      "sourceArticleId": "airavatesvara-temple-wp",
      "sourceGeohash4": "tcbq",
      "mediumPath": "_m/t/c/b/q/airavatesvara-temple-wp/one-hindu-temples-tami-medium.webp",
      "smallPath": "_m/t/c/b/q/airavatesvara-temple-wp/one-hindu-temples-tami-small.webp"
    },
